# LibreWolf isolated-process.patch  (Android)
# Owner: LW-M5-02 ("Enable isolatedProcess and the app zygote")
#
# Upstream file touched
# ----------------------
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/gecko/GeckoProvider.kt
#
# One hunk, two call arguments. Nothing else in any patch list touches this
# file (`grep -rn '^+++ b/mobile/' patches/` -> disable-data-reporting-android
# (moz.configure), no-nimbus (nimbus.fml.yaml, NimbusSetup.kt, HomeActivity.kt),
# neterror-jar, webgl-prompt-default, build-fixes, appservices-logins-addmany).
# So there is no ordering constraint against any patch that exists today. See
# "Ordering" below for the one that is coming.
#
#
# What LibreWolf wants instead, and why
# --------------------------------------
# On Android, Gecko's own content sandbox is NOT COMPILED IN. This is not an
# inference from the pref list, it is read out of the objdir of the very build
# this patch was verified on:
#
#   * `grep -i sandbox obj-x86_64/config.status` returns ONLY the seven RLBox
#     entries (MOZ_USING_WASM_SANDBOXING and MOZ_WASM_SANDBOXING_{EXPAT,
#     GRAPHITE,HUNSPELL,OGG,SOUNDTOUCH,WOFF2}). There is no MOZ_SANDBOX and no
#     MOZ_CONTENT_SANDBOX. RLBox sandboxes six media/text libraries inside a
#     process; it is not a process sandbox and does not confine content.
#   * `obj-x86_64/security/sandbox/` does not exist -- the directory is never
#     built for this target.
#
# So every `security.sandbox.*` pref is inert and there is no seccomp-bpf,
# namespace or capability confinement around a content process at all. That is
# what LW-M5-07's spike recorded, and it means the ONLY containment a
# LibreWolf-for-Android content process has is the containment Android itself
# provides.
#
# Android provides two mechanisms, and GeckoView already implements both:
#
#   android:isolatedProcess="true"  -- the service runs under its own, freshly
#       allocated isolated uid in the SELinux domain `isolated_app` instead of
#       `untrusted_app`. MEASURED HERE (see "How this was verified", items 1
#       and 2): the content process really does get `u:r:isolated_app:s0` and a
#       distinct uid, and the kernel really does refuse it access to
#       /data/data/org.mozilla.fenix.debug -- so a compromised content process
#       cannot read the profile, the cookie jar or the logins database, which
#       an `untrusted_app` content process running under the browser's own uid
#       trivially can. PLATFORM PROPERTY, documented by Android and NOT
#       separately measured here: an isolated uid can hold no Android
#       permissions and gets no supplementary groups, so it has no `inet` group
#       and cannot open sockets of its own, and it can reach almost no system
#       service. Nothing breaks: Gecko does its networking in the parent, and
#       the probe page's fetch() succeeded from inside the isolated process on
#       every run.
#
#   android:useAppZygote="true" + android:zygotePreloadName  -- content
#       processes are forked from an app-specific zygote that has already
#       dlopen()ed libmozglue, libxul and libnss3 (ZygotePreload.java), instead
#       of from the system zygote. The forked children share those relocated
#       pages copy-on-write.
#
# Both service families are ALREADY in the generated GeckoView manifest,
# unconditionally: mobile/android/geckoview/src/main/AndroidManifest_overlay.jinja
# emits `isolatedTab0..39` with android:isolatedProcess="true", one `zygoteTab`
# with isolatedProcess + useAppZygote, and android:zygotePreloadName on
# <application>. Verified in the built APK, not in the template -- `aapt2 dump
# xmltree --file AndroidManifest.xml fenix-x86_64-debug.apk` shows 89 services
# carrying an isolatedProcess attribute, the zygotePreloadName attribute and
# useAppZygote=true. Neither the --num-content-services nor the
# --enable-isolated-zygote-process mozconfig option gates any of it; the latter
# is read only by mobile/android/test_runner.
#
# So this patch adds no plumbing and no build flag. GeckoProvider.kt already
# called .isolatedProcessEnabled(...) and .appZygoteProcessEnabled(...); it
# passed the wrong values.
#
#
# Why the value is hard-coded here and NOT flipped in nimbus.fml.yaml
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two arguments used to read Settings.isIsolatedProcessEnabled and
# Settings.isAppZygoteEnabled (Settings.kt:980-992). Those are
# `booleanPreference` delegates whose default lambda is the Nimbus FML feature
# `isolated-content-processes`, and BOTH of its variables default to false
# (nimbus.fml.yaml:1040-1052, no per-channel `defaults:` block). Two ways to
# flip that were available:
#
#   (a) raise the two FML defaults to true;
#   (b) stop consulting the setting at all.
#
# (b) was chosen, for three reasons.
#
#   1. OWNERSHIP. nimbus.fml.yaml is a declared tree_path of LW-M4-03 and
#      LW-M5-01, not of LW-M5-02, whose only tree_path is GeckoProvider.kt. Two
#      patches editing one tree file must be ordered, not parallel
#      (docs/android/AGENTS.md). Editing the FML from here would be exactly the
#      collision the board's tree_paths exist to prevent.
#
#   2. IT IS THE STRONGER GUARANTEE, and this is a security decision. The
#      delegate is `booleanPreference(..., persistDefaultIfNotExists = false)`
#      (PreferencesHolder.kt:21-40, class BooleanPreference), so the FML default
#      is only consulted while the SharedPreferences key is ABSENT; the moment
#      anything writes that key -- today only SecretSettingsFragment.kt:408-418,
#      but landmine L2 is precisely about Fenix writing prefs at runtime -- the
#      stored value wins forever. And the FML value itself is an experiment-overridable channel:
#      FeatureHolder.value() calls getSdk()?.getVariables(featureId) first and
#      only falls back to the compiled-in default (FeatureHolder.kt:45-58). It
#      is true that LW-M4-03 has severed the remote half of that channel today,
#      but "safe because a different patch removed the attacker's transport" is
#      not a property this file can keep. A constant at the call site cannot be
#      lowered by an experiment, by a settings write, or by an upstream flip of
#      the FML default on the next rebase.
#
#   3. It keeps the whole change in one file that one task owns, so a future
#      rebase conflict is legible.
#
# The falsification test for claim 2 was run, not reasoned: with this patch
# applied, `pref_key_enable_isolated_process` and `pref_key_enable_app_zygote_process`
# were written as false into /data/data/org.mozilla.fenix.debug/shared_prefs/
# fenix_preferences.xml -- the exact artefact a settings write or an FML default
# produces -- and the content processes still came up isolated. On the
# unpatched build the mirror test (writing them true) turned isolation ON, which
# is what proves the mechanism itself works on this device and that the
# unpatched build's non-isolated processes really are the settings path's doing.
# Numbers and process lists are in "How this was verified" below.
#
#
# The app zygote has an API floor: SDK 29 (Android 10)
# -----------------------------------------------------
# The build's minSdk is 26 (build/moz.configure/android-sdk.configure:160 --
# nothing in assets/mozconfig.android overrides it), so Android 8.0/8.1/9
# devices are in range and are BELOW the floor. What happens there, from the
# tree:
#
#   * GeckoRuntimeSettings.Builder.appZygoteProcessEnabled
#     (GeckoRuntimeSettings.java:717-724) refuses the value itself:
#     `if (enabled && SDK_INT < Q) { Log.w(...
#     "Cannot set app Zygote preloading to true below SDK 29 (Android 10)!");
#     mAppZygoteProcess = false; return this; }`. So passing `true`
#     unconditionally is safe -- the guard is upstream's, not ours, and it will
#     track upstream if the floor ever moves.
#   * GeckoProcessType.determineContentProcessType() (:55-57) re-checks
#     SDK_INT >= Q before returning CONTENT_ISOLATED_WITH_ZYGOTE, so even a
#     stale `true` could not select the zygote service.
#   * isolatedProcess itself has NO floor and still applies. The fallback is
#     CONTENT_ISOLATED, whose manifest entries (isolatedTab0..39) carry
#     android:isolatedProcess="true" and are bound with the ordinary
#     Context.bindService() path (ServiceAllocator.DefaultBindDelegate), which
#     has produced isolated uids since API 16.
#   * The one thing that IS lost below 29 is the unbounded instance count:
#     ServiceAllocator.allocate() picks IsolatedContentPolicy only when
#     hasQApis() (:553-558), so pre-29 devices use DefaultContentPolicy and are
#     capped at MOZ_ANDROID_CONTENT_SERVICE_COUNT (40) simultaneous content
#     processes instead of Android-unlimited-with-instanceName. 40 is far above
#     dom.ipc.processCount (8), so this is not a practical limit.
#
# Net effect below API 29: isolated uids yes, app zygote no, no crash, no
# silent loss of isolation. Verified on a real Android 9 (API 28) emulator --
# see "How this was verified".
#
#
# What this patch knowingly leaves inconsistent
# ----------------------------------------------
# Two upstream call sites still read the Settings values, and neither can be
# touched from here (Settings.kt and SecretSettingsFragment.kt are not this
# task's tree_paths):
#
#   * SecretSettingsFragment.kt:408-418 -- the two hidden debug-menu switches.
#     They are `isVisible = Config.channel.isNightlyOrDebug`, so on the RELEASE
#     build LibreWolf ships they do not exist at all. On a debug build they
#     still render (unchecked, because they read the FML default) and writing
#     them no longer changes anything. Cosmetic, debug-only, and the honest
#     trade for point 2 above.
#   * FenixApplication.kt:1089-1090 -- two Glean metrics that would now report
#     the settings value rather than the effective one. Glean is removed
#     wholesale by LW-M4-01; flagged so that task does not "fix" it by
#     re-plumbing the setting.
#
#
# Ordering
# ---------
# No constraint today. LW-M5-01 (fission-isolation.patch) is the one to watch:
# its brief talks about GeckoProvider.kt's `shouldUseNimbus` gate, which sits
# immediately below this hunk, while its declared tree_paths are only
# nimbus.fml.yaml and Core.kt. If LW-M5-01 does end up editing GeckoProvider.kt
# it becomes a shared-file pair with this patch and needs a row in the ordering
# table in docs/android/AGENTS.md plus a tree_paths fix on the board. Reported.
#
#
# Nothing happens outside this diff (landmine L4)
# ------------------------------------------------
# No file copies, no rm, no generated file edited by hand, no manifest edit --
# the isolated and zygote service definitions were already in the manifest the
# ordinary build generates, which is the whole reason this patch is two lines
# of Kotlin.
#
#
# HOW THIS WAS VERIFIED, AND WHAT WAS NOT
# ----------------------------------------
# Everything below comes from a RUNNING APK, not from reading the tree.
#
# Artefacts. scripts/android-apk.sh built two fenix-x86_64-debug.apk from the
# SAME patched tree (24 common + 6 android patches, including LW-M4-03's
# no-nimbus, so fission isolationStrategy is 2 in both), differing ONLY by this
# patch. MOZ_BUILD_DATE 20260816204534, GeckoView from ~/lw-m2-03/aar.
#
# Devices. Android emulator, `default` (non-Google) x86_64 system images:
#   API 30 / Android 11, 4 vCPU, 3057 MB RAM, -no-window -no-audio -gpu host
#   API 28 / Android 9,  4 vCPU, 3072 MB RAM   (the below-the-floor case)
# Host: 32 core / 62 GB Fedora, NOT idle -- a second emulator belonging to
# another agent ran throughout. Absolute milliseconds are therefore inflated;
# the comparisons are between conditions measured on the same device minutes
# apart, and every condition was repeated.
#
# 1. THE ACCEPTANCE CRITERION, on API 30, stock install, nothing configured:
#
#    $ adb shell ps -A -Z | grep -iE 'fenix|zygote'
#    u:r:untrusted_app:s0:c135,...  u0_a135  12883  293  ... org.mozilla.fenix.debug
#    u:r:app_zygote:s0:c512,c768    u0_a135  13036  293  ... org.mozilla.fenix.debug_zygote
#    u:r:isolated_app:s0:c512,c768  u0_i0    13047 13036 ... :zygoteTab_disable_art_image_:...
#    u:r:untrusted_app:s0:c135,...  u0_a135  13081  293  ... :gpu_disable_art_image_
#    u:r:isolated_app:s0:c512,c768  u0_i1    13175 13036 ... :zygoteTab_disable_art_image_:...
#    u:r:isolated_app:s0:c512,c768  u0_i2    13224 13036 ... :zygoteTab_disable_art_image_:...
#    u:r:isolated_app:s0:c512,c768  u0_i3    13286 13036 ... :zygoteTab_disable_art_image_:...
#
#    Every content process: SELinux domain `isolated_app`, its own isolated uid
#    (u0_i0..u0_i3), PPID 13036 = the app zygote. The app zygote itself is
#    present, domain `app_zygote`. The GPU process correctly stays
#    `untrusted_app` -- only content is isolated. The board's verify line
#    (`ps -A -Z | grep -c isolated`) returns 0 on the unpatched build and 3-4 on
#    this one.
#
# 2. THE ISOLATION IS REAL, not just a label. Same device (API 28), same URL,
#    same code path, only the APK differs:
#      patched  : 14 kernel denials of the form
#                 avc: denied { getattr } for path="/data/data/org.mozilla.fenix.debug"
#                 scontext=u:r:isolated_app:s0:c0,... tcontext=u:object_r:app_data_file:s0:c67,...
#                 tclass=dir permissive=0
#      unpatched: 0 such denials (the content process runs as the browser's own
#                 uid and simply reads it)
#    i.e. the content process is refused access to the profile directory by the
#    kernel, and the browser works anyway -- Gecko gets what it needs over IPC.
#
# 3. THE VALUES CANNOT BE LOWERED (the "survive a Nimbus fetch" criterion). On
#    the patched build, both keys were written FALSE into
#    /data/data/org.mozilla.fenix.debug/shared_prefs/fenix_preferences.xml --
#    the exact artefact a Fenix settings write, the secret-settings toggle or an
#    FML default produces -- and the app relaunched:
#      <boolean name="pref_key_enable_isolated_process" value="false" />
#      <boolean name="pref_key_enable_app_zygote_process" value="false" />
#    Result: still 4 isolated_app content processes and the app zygote. The
#    mirror control on the UNPATCHED build (writing them TRUE) turned isolation
#    on, which is what proves the mechanism works on this device and that the
#    unpatched build's non-isolated processes really are the settings path's
#    doing rather than something else.
#
# 4. BELOW THE API FLOOR -- run on a real Android 9 (API 28) emulator with this
#    same APK:
#      W GeckoRuntimeSettings: Cannot set app Zygote preloading to true below SDK 29 (Android 10)!
#      u:r:isolated_app:s0:c0,...  u0_i0  3130  1733 ... :isolatedTab_disable_art_image_12
#      u:r:isolated_app:s0:c1,...  u0_i1  3195  1733 ... :isolatedTab_disable_art_image_34
#      u:r:isolated_app:s0:c2,...  u0_i2  3315  1733 ... :isolatedTab_disable_art_image_6
#    No app_zygote process exists; PPID 1733 is the SYSTEM zygote; the content
#    processes are the plain `isolatedTab<N>` services and are still isolated.
#    Exactly the documented degradation, and the page loaded normally.
#
# 5. COST. Nine reboot-isolated runs on API 30. Each run: reboot -> uninstall ->
#    install -> one warm-up launch -> 8 cold starts (`am start-activity -W -S`,
#    force-stop between; medians below drop the first two, which are ART
#    warm-up) -> 4 cold "launch to page JavaScript finished" timings against a
#    local HTTP server -> a fixed 9-tab workload across 3 origins -> 20 s settle
#    -> `dumpsys meminfo <pid>` summed over every process of the app.
#
#      condition                    runs  cold start   first page   sum PSS
#      -------------------------------------------------------------------
#      unpatched (no isolation)        3      520 ms       830 ms   567.7 MiB
#      isolatedProcess only            1      474 ms       726 ms   565.3 MiB
#      isolated + app zygote (THIS)    4      518 ms       830 ms   546.5 MiB
#
#    Cold start and first page are pooled medians (n = 18/24 and 12/16); sum PSS
#    is the mean over runs, with 4 content processes + parent + GPU in every
#    condition (+ the zygote where enabled).
#
#    * STARTUP COST IS ZERO within this rig's noise. 518 ms vs 520 ms; the
#      spread between repeats of the SAME condition (474-534 ms across the
#      unpatched and isolated-only runs) is larger than the difference between
#      conditions. First page is identical to the millisecond-median, 830 vs 830.
#    * MEMORY IS A NET SAVING of 21.2 MiB (-3.7 %), and it is entirely the app
#      zygote's: isolatedProcess on its own moves nothing (565.3 vs 567.7 MiB),
#      while adding the zygote drops each content process from ~66 MiB to
#      ~57.5 MiB PSS -- about 8.4 MiB saved per content process, against 8.5 MiB
#      for the zygote process itself. System-wide `dumpsys meminfo` "Used RAM"
#      agrees: 1476.8 -> 1460.6 MiB.
#    * FOR LW-M5-01: isolatedProcess costs you nothing to keep, and the app
#      zygote pays for itself from the second content process onwards. At
#      dom.ipc.processCount = 8 (modules/libpref/init/all.js:1866) the
#      extrapolated saving is ~8.4 x 8 - 8.5 = ~59 MiB, which is headroom for
#      the 1-vs-2 isolationStrategy decision rather than a cost against it.
#      Do not, however, reuse these numbers as a device budget: they are an
#      x86_64 emulator with a 172 MB libxul.
#
# 6. NO FUNCTIONAL REGRESSION. A probe page reported its own results back to the
#    server on every one of 30 page loads across both builds; all fields are
#    byte-identical between the unpatched and patched runs:
#      canvas2d=255, webgl="WebGL 2.0 | Generic Renderer", wasm=true,
#      localStorage ok, IndexedDB ok, Web Worker ok, fetch ok,
#      hardwareConcurrency=4
#    Audio was checked separately because an isolated uid is the obvious thing
#    for AudioFlinger to refuse: on BOTH API 28 and API 30 the isolated content
#    process created an AudioContext (state suspended, 44100 Hz -- suspended is
#    the correct answer with no user gesture) and logcat shows AudioFlinger
#    accepting the client ("Client defaulted notificationFrames to 882 for
#    frameCount 1956"). Playback through real speakers was NOT tested; the
#    emulator ran with -no-audio.
#
# METHODOLOGY WARNING, because it nearly produced a wrong answer here. The first
# A/B was run as two measurements back to back on one long-lived emulator boot,
# and it reported cold start 536 -> 2121 ms and first page 878 -> 4289 ms, i.e.
# a 4x regression. That was ENTIRELY device drift: repeating the UNPATCHED build
# immediately afterwards reproduced the same ~2100 ms. Only rebooting the device
# before every condition made the numbers reproducible, and then the difference
# vanished. Any future startup measurement on this rig must reboot between
# conditions and must repeat the control after the treatment.
#
# NOT VERIFIED:
#   * Any physical device. Everything above is an x86_64 emulator.
#   * armv7/arm64 APKs. The two APKs were built for all three ABIs from the same
#     Gradle run, but only x86_64 was executed.
#   * The RELEASE variant. The APK is `assembleDebug` (channel `developer`).
#     This patch has no channel-dependent behaviour -- it is a literal `true` at
#     the call site, and unlike the FML route there is no per-channel generation
#     step that could differ -- but the release build has not been run.
#   * More than 4 simultaneous content processes. dom.ipc.processCount is 8; the
#     9-tab/3-origin workload produced 4. The per-process figures extrapolate,
#     the totals do not.
#   * Audio actually reaching a speaker, and WebRTC capture (camera/microphone
#     permissions cannot be held by an isolated uid; capture is brokered by the
#     parent, but that path was not exercised).
#
--- a/mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/gecko/GeckoProvider.kt
+++ b/mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/gecko/GeckoProvider.kt
